<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuan Shao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/202">202</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuan Shao was a Chinese warlord of the late Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, from a family of high officials. He led the coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> in 190 and became master of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, allying with Wuhuan chiefs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadun">Tadun</a>. His defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-guandu">Battle of Guandu</a> in 200 left the north to Cao Cao, and he died in 202.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shao.jpg" alt="Yuan Shao" width="297" height="586" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Plastictv at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_Shao_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yuan Shao, courtesy name Benchu, was born in Ruyang County in Runan Commandery. His family had held high positions in the Han civil service for over four generations, and he was a great-grandson of Yuan An. His birth was a source of dispute with his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a>, since his mother had been a servant of Yuan Feng; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a> hold instead that he was an older cousin adopted by Yuan Cheng. Yuan Shao helped save some of the partisans during the Disasters of Partisan Prohibitions, and he later served as an aide to General-in-Chief He Jin, who trusted him deeply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Emperor Ling died in May 189, He Jin and Yuan Shao plotted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, and He Jin summoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a>'s troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. He Jin was assassinated on 22 September 189. Dong Zhuo then seized the capital, and when Yuan Shao opposed his plan to depose Emperor Shao he fled to Ji Province. In early 190 a coalition of regional officials, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a>, formed behind him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, but the coalition's leaders, disunited, soon went home. Yuan Shao took Ji Province from Han Fu in 191 and built a state at Ye, and in the winter of that year he defeated Gongsun Zan's cavalry at the Battle of Jieqiao using massed crossbowmen.</p><p>In 195 Ju Shou urged him to welcome Emperor Xian, but Guo Tu and Chunyu Qiong opposed it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> took the emperor to Xuchang instead. After destroying Gongsun Zan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a> in 199, Yuan Shao held the four provinces north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and allied with the Wuhuan tribes. In 200 he marched against Cao Cao with more than 100,000 veteran troops and many militia. At Guandu, Cao Cao burned his supply depot at Wuchao, officers including Zhang He and Gao Lan defected, and Yuan Shao fled north with hundreds of men. Cao Cao defeated him again at Cangting in 201.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yuan Shao put down rebellions in his domain and restored order, but he died on 28 June 202 without choosing between his sons Yuan Tan and Yuan Shang. Yuan Shang took the succession with the support of Shen Pei, Pang Ji and Lady Liu, and the sons then fought each other. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> eliminated Yuan Shao's heirs and remnants by 207. After taking Ye in 204 he wept at Yuan Shao's tomb and gave the family gifts and a pension. His failure, despite his family and position, was commonly blamed on indecisiveness and his inability to heed his advisers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yuan Shao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Warlords then fight for territory: Cao Cao defeats <b>Yuan Shao</b> at Guandu in the summer of 200, and Liu Bei, with his oath brothers Guan Yu and Zhang Fei, and the strategist Zhuge Liang join Sun Quan to defeat Cao Cao at the Battle of Red Cliffs.</blockquote><a href="/work/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Cao Cao, holding the last Han emperor, Emperor Xian, in his power, reunified northern China after defeating <b>Yuan Shao</b> at Guandu in 200, and became King of Wei in 216.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 2nd century Wuhuan chiefs such as Tadun allied with the warlord <b>Yuan Shao</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/wuhuan">Wuhuan</a></li><li><blockquote>After he was defeated by Lü Bu and Cao Cao and his territory collapsed, Yuan Shu set out in 199 to join his cousin <b>Yuan Shao</b> in the north, was blocked and died, reportedly asking in vain for honey water.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhongjia">Zhongjia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shangshui County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Linzhang County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.54,114.61&amp;year=192" aria-label="Shangshui County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yuan Shao"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4616 4688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4616 4688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.54,114.61&amp;year=192">What was here in 192 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
